WEBVTT

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:42.590
<v Ira Glass>I had a weird conversation with a congressman this week. This is Mark Pocan, Democrat out of Wisconsin. The main sponsor of a bill to do this thing that protesters have been calling for the last few months, abolish ICE, the Immigration and Customs Enforcement agency. His bill would terminate the agency. Immigration enforcement would continue, but a bipartisan commission would hand off the stuff that ICE does to other federal agencies, and eliminate anything it deems inhumane or cruel, or a violation of international agreements. After one year, the agency would not exist. It would be abolished. But the politics of this are so peculiar that in our half hour conversation, the congressman could not bring himself to say the actual words that he would abolish ICE.
<v Ira Glass>这周我和一位国会议员进行了一次奇怪的谈话。这位议员是来自威斯康星州的民主党人马克·波坎（Mark Pocan）。他是众议院一项法案的主要发起人，该法案旨在实现抗议者过去几个月一直在呼吁的目标：废除美国移民与海关执法局（ICE）。他的法案将终止该机构。移民执法工作将继续进行，但一个两党委员会将把ICE负责的事务移交给其他联邦机构，并取消任何被认为不人道、残酷或违反国际协议的职能。一年后，该机构将不复存在。它将被废除。但这背后的政治博弈极其古怪，以至于在我们半小时的谈话中，这位议员始终无法说出“我会废除ICE”这样的话。

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:45.170
<v Ira Glass>Do you want to abolish the agency?
<v Ira Glass>你想废除这个机构吗？

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:47.570
<v Mark Pocan>I think I've answered that question a few times, Ira.
<v Mark Pocan>艾拉，我想这个问题我已经回答过几次了。

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:50.750
<v Ira Glass>But just yes or no. Do you want to abolish the agency?
<v Ira Glass>但请只回答是或否。你想废除这个机构吗？

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:59.120
<v Mark Pocan>Ira, for a half hour, I've given you a conversation on this, and I think I've explained what I'm trying to do. Do you have the bill, because do you need me to shoot you the words. I'm not sure if you have it in front of you.
<v Mark Pocan>艾拉，我已经就这个问题和你谈了半个小时了，我想我已经解释了我想做的事情。你有那份法案吗？如果你需要我把文字念给你听的话。我不确定你是否把它放在面前。

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:04.940
<v Ira Glass>No, I have it in front of me. It says to establish a commission that would terminate Immigration and Customs Enforcement.
<v Ira Glass>不，我有。上面写着成立一个委员会，终止移民与海关执法局。

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:17.810
<v Mark Pocan>It's establish a commission to reassign the essential duties that were created by ICE to other agencies, and to get rid of the unnecessary duties. That's the essential thrust of the bill, and that's what we're trying to accomplish.
<v Mark Pocan>是成立一个委员会，将ICE创造的必要职责重新分配给其他机构，并剔除不必要的职责。这就是该法案的核心目的，也是我们试图实现的。

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:23.480
<v Ira Glass>Right. So you do want to terminate the agency? You do want to abolish the agency?
<v Ira Glass>对。所以你确实想终止这个机构？你确实想废除它？

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:25.730
<v Mark Pocan>I want to make sure the essential duties of the agency--
<v Mark Pocan>我想确保该机构的基本职责——

00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:46.870
<v Ira Glass>To understand why he was talking this way, let me tell you the story of his last two weeks in Congress. It began when he proposed this bill to abolish ICE. Mainly, he and his co-sponsors were objecting to the way that President Trump has the agency going after undocumented immigrants these days. One of his co-sponsors, Congresswoman Pramila Jayapal told me they had no illusions about this ever making it to the floor of the House.
<v Ira Glass>为了理解他为什么要这样说话，让我告诉你们他过去两周在国会的经历。这一切始于他提出废除ICE的法案。主要是因为他和联合发起人们反对特朗普总统目前利用该机构针对无证移民的方式。其中一位联合发起人，普拉米拉·贾亚帕尔（Pramila Jayapal）众议员告诉我，他们对这项法案能被提交到众议院表决不抱任何幻想。

00:01:46.870 --> 00:02:10.370
<v Pramila Jayapal>No, no, no. Because no immigration bills on our side get brought to the floor, not even ones that have significant co-sponsorship, including Republican co-sponsorship, like the Dream Act. And so it was very clear to us that this was just a messaging bill for us to be able to really talk about how outrageous the agency is.
<v Pramila Jayapal>不，不，不。因为我们这边的移民法案根本不会被提交表决，即使是那些有重要共同发起人，包括共和党共同发起人的法案，比如《梦想法案》（Dream Act），也不会被提交。所以对我们来说很清楚，这只是一项用来传达信息的法案，让我们能够真正讨论这个机构是多么令人愤慨。

00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:18.350
<v Ira Glass>So they introduced their bill, and it got some Democratic support. But you know who really loved it? Republicans.
<v Ira Glass>于是他们提出了法案，并得到了一些民主党人的支持。但你知道谁最喜欢它吗？共和党人。

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:20.170
<v Paul Ryan>They have really jumped the sharks on the left.
<v Paul Ryan>左派真的已经走火入魔了。

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:22.820
<v Ira Glass>This is the Speaker of the House, Paul Ryan.
<v Ira Glass>这是众议院议长保罗·瑞安（Paul Ryan）。

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:33.860
<v Paul Ryan>They have really-- it's an amazing, it's the craziest position I've ever seen. And they are just tripping over themselves to move too far to the left. They're out of the mainstream of America.
<v Paul Ryan>他们真的——这是我见过最疯狂的立场。他们只是急于表现得过于左倾。他们已经脱离了美国的主流。

00:02:33.860 --> 00:03:05.650
<v Ira Glass>For months now, actually, conservative media have loved any hint that the Democrats want to abolish ICE. Fits into their whole narrative that the Democrats are soft on immigration, and adds a kind of wingnut extremist edge to it, like people who call for the abolition of the Internal Revenue Service. In that way, abolish ICE is the build the wall of the left, blunt like a punch to the jaw, exciting for those who believe in it, whackadoodle for those who don't. And if you get right down to it, maybe not the very best way to fix the problem that it is supposed to fix, but that is not what we're here talking about today.
<v Ira Glass>几个月来，保守派媒体一直乐于捕捉任何关于民主党想要废除ICE的暗示。这符合他们关于民主党对移民问题立场软弱的叙事，并给它增加了一种极端主义色彩，就像那些呼吁废除美国国税局的人一样。从这个意义上说，“废除ICE”就像左派的“修建隔离墙”，像重拳一样直白，对那些信仰它的人来说很刺激，对不信的人来说则很疯狂。如果深究起来，这也许并不是解决问题的最好办法，但这并不是我们今天讨论的主题。

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:09.380
<v Ira Glass>Here are Jesse Watters and Laura Ingraham, gleeful on Fox.
<v Ira Glass>这是福克斯新闻的杰西·沃特斯（Jesse Watters）和劳拉·英格拉汉姆（Laura Ingraham），他们幸灾乐祸。

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:13.250
<v Jesse Watters>I cannot believe they're doing it. It's political suicide.
<v Jesse Watters>我无法相信他们竟然这么做。这是政治自杀。

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:18.670
<v Laura Ingraham>Good luck on turning that into a platform that actually wins votes.
<v Laura Ingraham>祝他们能把这个变成一个真正能赢得选票的纲领。

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:18.790
<v Ira Glass>President Trump tweeted, "The two biggest opponents of ICE in America today are  the Democratic Party  and MS-13!"
<v Ira Glass>特朗普总统发推文说：“今天美国ICE最大的两个反对者是民主党和MS-13帮派！”

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:19.160
<v Donald Trump>I love that issue if they're going to actually do that. They're seriously talking about that?
<v Donald Trump>如果他们真要这么做，我太喜欢这个议题了。他们是认真的吗？

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:28.400
<v Ira Glass>It didn't take long, just one day. The Republican leadership in the House was like, you want to abolish ICE? Great. Here's House Majority Leader, Kevin McCarthy.
<v Ira Glass>没花多长时间，仅仅一天。众议院共和党领导层就像是说，你们想废除ICE？太好了。这是众议院多数党领袖凯文·麦卡锡（Kevin McCarthy）。

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:32.260
<v Kevin Mccarthy>Well, you know what? We're going to have a vote on the floor.
<v Kevin Mccarthy>好吧，你知道吗？我们要进行投票。

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:44.300
<v Ira Glass>So Mark Pocan and a few of his co-sponsors did something you do not see very often. They told everybody, we have this bill, don't vote for it. Basically, it's a trap.
<v Ira Glass>所以马克·波坎和他的几位共同发起人做了一件你很少见到的事情。他们告诉所有人：我们有这个法案，但别投票支持它。基本上，这是一个陷阱。

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:53.180
<v Mark Pocan>Clearly, there was no real intention to see that bill pass. They were trying to use it as some kind of a gotcha at election time.
<v Mark Pocan>很明显，他们并没有真正打算让法案通过。他们只是想在选举时用它作为一个陷阱。

00:03:53.180 --> 00:04:14.720
<v Ira Glass>Apparently, the Republicans saw this, that all the Democrats were ready to go on record and vote, all of them together, and say they do not want to abolish ICE, which is exactly the opposite message that the Republicans want them to be carrying into midterms. So they ditched that plan. The bill didn't come to the fore. Instead, this week, Republicans in the House took a different tack.
<v Ira Glass>显然，共和党人看穿了这一点，他们发现所有民主党人都准备好投票表态，所有人一起站出来说他们不想废除ICE，这与共和党希望他们在中期选举中传达的信息正好相反。所以他们放弃了那个计划。该法案没有被提交表决。相反，本周，众议院的共和党人采取了不同的策略。

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:17.210
<v Man 1>The clerk will report the title of the resolution.
<v Man 1>书记官将宣读决议标题。

00:04:17.210 --> 00:04:28.300
<v Clerk>House resolution 990, resolution supporting the officers and personnel who carry out the important mission of the United States Immigration and Customs Enforcement.
<v Clerk>众议院第990号决议案，支持执行美国移民与海关执法局重要使命的官员和人员。

00:04:28.300 --> 00:05:11.600
<v Ira Glass>Behold, a House resolution in support of ICE and also incidentally officially denouncing anybody who calls for the agency's abolishment. Calls to abolish ICE, it says, are an insult to these heroic law enforcement officers who make sacrifices every day to secure our borders, enforce our laws, and protect our safety and security. No surprise it passed easily, 244 to 35. Most Democrats, 133 of them, simply voted present, and thus avoided being on the record either for or against the agency. That is just how radioactive, how politically dangerous this whole idea of abolishing ICE is. The phrase, abolish ICE, does not even appear in the Democrats' bill to abolish ICE.
<v Ira Glass>看，这是一项支持ICE的众议院决议，顺便正式谴责任何呼吁废除该机构的人。决议称，废除ICE的呼声是对这些英勇的执法人员的侮辱，他们每天为了保卫边境、执行法律以及保护我们的安全而做出牺牲。毫无意外，该决议以244票对35票轻松通过。大多数民主党人，共133人，仅仅投了“出席”票，从而避免了在是否支持该机构的问题上留下记录。这就是“废除ICE”这个想法在政治上有多么危险和敏感。“废除ICE”这个词组甚至没有出现在民主党人废除ICE的法案中。

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:37.820
<v Ira Glass>And I suppose that's why Congressman Pocan did not want to say the phrase out loud with me. Well, from WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today on our program, we have two stories from this hot potato of a federal agency, one that does so many things I bet you know nothing about, one that has expanded its powers rapidly. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>我想这就是为什么波坎议员不想和我当面说出这个词组的原因。这里是来自芝加哥WBEZ的《美国生活》（This American Life），我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。今天在我们的节目中，我们有两个关于这个烫手联邦机构的故事，它做的事情多到我敢说你一无所知，而且它的权力在迅速扩张。请继续收听。

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:41.510
<v Ira Glass>Act One, We'll Talk About It in the Car.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，我们在车里谈谈。

00:05:41.510 --> 00:06:13.130
<v Ira Glass>So in this show about ICE, before we get started, it seems like it might be a good idea to do a very quick primer on what exactly ICE is. The agency has only been around for 15 years. It was created after September 11th. The idea was, Customs and Border Protection would be the agency at the actual border stopping people there. ICE would deal with the interior of the country, and a lot of its mission at the beginning was very post-9/11. It was all about catching terrorists, arms traffickers, cyber crimes, transnational gangs, child predators, catching criminals of various sorts.
<v Ira Glass>关于这个关于ICE的节目，在开始之前，先简单科普一下ICE到底是什么似乎是个好主意。该机构成立才15年。它是在“9·11”事件后成立的。最初的想法是，海关及边境保卫局（CBP）负责在边境拦截人员，而ICE则负责处理美国国内事务。起初它的许多使命都是针对“9·11”后的情况，全是关于打击恐怖分子、军火贩运者、网络犯罪、跨国帮派、虐童分子，抓捕各种罪犯。

00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:47.410
<v Ira Glass>One department of ICE went after undocumented immigrants. But it was not nearly as robust. It did not do as much until after immigration reform failed in 2007. Since then, that part of the agency has stepped up its game under rules set by each new administration. President Obama still has the record for the most people deported, but President Trump, of course, has unleashed agents like they have never been unleashed before. Today, they can question pretty much anybody they meet and put them into deportation proceedings, even if they have no criminal record, even if they have lived here productively and peacefully for decades.
<v Ira Glass>ICE的一个部门负责打击无证移民。但它远没有那么强硬。在2007年移民改革失败之前，它并没有做那么多工作。从那时起，在每一届新政府制定的规则下，该机构的那部分职能一直在加强。奥巴马总统仍然保持着驱逐出境人数最多的纪录，但特朗普总统当然已经像从未有过的程度那样“释放”了这些特工。今天，他们几乎可以盘问遇到的任何人，并将他们送入驱逐程序，即使他们没有犯罪记录，即使他们已经在生产、平静地生活了几十年。

00:06:47.410 --> 00:07:20.260
<v Ira Glass>ICE has brought back worksite raids. They're involved with the family separations, along with Customs and Border Protection. This week, there were, and of course it's a unique week, because every day, families that the Trump administration separated at the border are being reunited. The administration has a court imposed deadline to get everybody back together by Thursday, July 26. That's why they acknowledged that they are not going to make it, or maybe even come close, which brings us to our first story today. And OK, I realize there is a lot of coverage out there of parents being reunited with children.
<v Ira Glass>ICE恢复了工地突袭行动。他们与海关及边境保卫局一起参与了家庭分离。本周，当然这是非常特殊的一周，因为每天都有被特朗普政府在边境拆散的家庭正在团聚。政府设定了7月26日星期四的法院强制期限，要求让所有人团聚。这就是为什么他们承认他们无法完成任务，甚至连接近目标都做不到，这引出了我们今天的第一段故事。好的，我知道关于父母与孩子团聚的报道很多。

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:53.890
<v Ira Glass>But we came across this audio that we thought was unusual, and took us inside the world of that in this way that we had not heard elsewhere. A reporter named Jeremy Raff, who's with The Atlantic, has been following this one lawyer in Texas for this documentary he's making. And he's friends with one of the producers of our show, Nadia Reiman and shared with her this material that he's been videotaping of this lawyer trying to reunite one mom and her son. Quick warning that we've unbeeped the curse words here in the podcast. If you prefer a beeped version of our show, maybe you're listening with kids, it's at our website. Here's Nadia and you will hear from Jeremy, the reporter, later in the story.
<v Ira Glass>但我们偶然发现了一段录音，我们认为它很特别，带我们进入了那个世界，这是我们在别处从未听说过的。一位名叫杰里米·拉夫（Jeremy Raff）的《大西洋月刊》记者一直在跟踪报道德克萨斯州的一位律师，为他正在制作的纪录片做准备。他和我们节目的制作人之一纳迪亚·雷曼（Nadia Reiman）是朋友，并将他拍摄的这位律师试图让一位母亲和她儿子团聚的视频资料分享给了她。提前警告一下，我们在播客中没有对脏话进行消音处理。如果你喜欢消音版，或者你是和孩子们一起听，请访问我们的网站。这是纳迪亚，稍后你会在故事中听到记者杰里米的讲述。

00:07:53.890 --> 00:08:05.540
<v Nadia Reiman>Jodi Goodwin, the immigration lawyer, speaks pretty good Spanish for a white girl. She's effortless in it, moves through her words in neatly accented Norteno tones, dropping perfectly placed Spanish f-bombs.
<v Nadia Reiman>移民律师乔迪·古德温（Jodi Goodwin）对一个白人女孩来说，西班牙语说得非常好。她说得毫不费力，用带有些许北方口音的语调流畅地表达，还会穿插恰到好处的西班牙语脏话。

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:15.960
<v Jodi Goodwin>[SPEAKING SPANISH] dialing numbers, you know, just waiting for somebody to answer the phone. How are you doing?
<v Jodi Goodwin>[西班牙语] 正在拨号，你知道，只是等着有人接电话。你怎么样？

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:28.180
<v Nadia Reiman>That's her on the phone with an ICE officer. She's in Harlingen, Texas, way down in the southern tip, where she's been practicing immigration law for more than 20 years. So she knows these guys. She's grown up with them.
<v Nadia Reiman>那是她正在和一位ICE官员通电话。她位于德克萨斯州的哈灵根，地处最南端，她在那里从事移民法律工作已经超过20年了。所以她认识这些人。她是和他们一起成长的。

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:48.380
<v Jodi Goodwin>I mean I care about them. I mean, they're my friends. They're people that I work with all the time. I mean, we've known each other for like over two decades. We've seen our kids be born, we've gone through health issues with our children. The kids go to the same schools.
<v Jodi Goodwin>我是说，我在乎他们。我的意思是，他们是我的朋友。他们是我一直共事的人。我们认识都超过二十年了。我们看着彼此的孩子出生，我们一起经历了孩子们的健康问题。孩子们上同一所学校。

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:50.470
<v Jodi Goodwin>Yeah, yeah, I'm here.
<v Jodi Goodwin>对，对，我在这儿。

00:08:50.470 --> 00:09:03.160
<v Nadia Reiman>She's on the phone trying to get a Honduran woman named Anita out of detention and reunited with her son, Jenri. They have been separated for about a month. An ICE agent told her that Anita should be out on bond, but she's not.
<v Nadia Reiman>她正在打电话试图让一位名叫安妮塔（Anita）的洪都拉斯妇女从拘留中心获释，并与她的儿子詹里（Jenri）团聚。他们已经分离大约一个月了。一位ICE探员告诉她，安妮塔应该获准保释，但她并没有出来。

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:25.600
<v Jodi Goodwin>Are you freaking kidding me? I know. This woman has a freaking five year old. OK, who's my best bet with the supervisors to get this overridden? Because this is bullshit to have one policy for some people and continue to cause prolonged trauma to a fucking five year old.
<v Jodi Goodwin>你在开玩笑吗？我知道。这个女人有个五岁的孩子。好了，我找哪个主管能推翻这个决定？因为对一些人实行一种政策，却继续对一个该死的五岁孩子造成长期创伤，这简直是胡扯。

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:56.860
<v Nadia Reiman>Even the ICE agents that Jodi's known for over two decades have conflicting info. One says, yeah, she should be out soon on bond. The other is like, no, we're not giving bonds anymore. But Jodi talks to a manager, and she gets them to release Anita. Anita's kid, Jenri, though, he's at a shelter nearby. It's called BCFS. To get him out, Jodi has his birth certificate and Anita's ID. She's emailed all the necessary paperwork, but she's still not sure how it will play out.
<v Nadia Reiman>即使是乔迪认识了二十多年的ICE探员，也提供相互矛盾的信息。一个说，是的，她很快就能保释。另一个说，不，我们不再提供保释了。但乔迪和一位经理谈了谈，她让他们释放了安妮塔。不过，安妮塔的孩子詹里还在附近的一家收容所里。它叫BCFS。为了接他出来，乔迪带了他的出生证明和安妮塔的身份证。她通过电子邮件发送了所有必要的文件，但她仍然不确定结果会怎样。

00:09:56.860 --> 00:10:28.710
<v Nadia Reiman>She's seen other parents do this, and be told, you can visit for an hour, but you can't have your child back. Come back again tomorrow. One parent had been told this four times. Four days in a row, they showed up, hung out with their kid for an hour, and still, no reunification. So recently, some parents have been buying tents and threatening to set up a camp right outside the shelters, refusing to leave until they get their kids back. So Jodi tells Anita about this and says, do you want to sleep outside if that's what it takes to get Jenri back?
<v Nadia Reiman>她见过其他父母这样做，结果却被告知：你可以探视一小时，但不能把孩子领回去。明天再来。有一位家长被告知过四次。连续四天，他们出现并和孩子待了一个小时，结果仍然没有团聚。所以最近，一些父母开始买帐篷，威胁要在收容所外扎营，拒绝离开，直到找回孩子。所以乔迪告诉安妮塔这些情况，问道：如果必须睡在外面才能接回詹里，你愿意吗？

00:10:28.710 --> 00:10:34.160
<v Jodi Goodwin>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Jodi Goodwin>[西班牙语]

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:34.790
<v Anita>Si.
<v Anita>是的。

00:10:34.790 --> 00:10:58.450
<v Nadia Reiman>Anita's like, yes. While Jodi is explaining all of this to Anita, she's going through this huge stack of papers. She turns one page, and there is a black and white photo of Anita and her son. It was taken just a few moments before border patrol separated them. Anita sees this picture, and starts to tear up. Jodi notices.
<v Nadia Reiman>安妮塔说，愿意。当乔迪向安妮塔解释这一切时，她正在翻阅一大叠文件。她翻过一页，上面有一张安妮塔和儿子的黑白照片。那是边境巡逻队将他们分开前几分钟拍的。安妮塔看到这张照片，开始流泪。乔迪注意到了。

00:10:58.450 --> 00:11:01.670
<v Jodi Goodwin>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Jodi Goodwin>[西班牙语]

00:11:01.670 --> 00:11:06.330
<v Nadia Reiman>And she's like, hey, we're in this fight together.
<v Nadia Reiman>她像是说，嘿，我们是一起在战斗。

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:10.270
<v Jodi Goodwin>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Jodi Goodwin>[西班牙语]

00:11:10.270 --> 00:11:13.120
<v Nadia Reiman>Those tears, we're going to save those tears for when you get your kid back.
<v Nadia Reiman>那些眼泪，我们要把它们留到你找回孩子的时候再流。

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:16.000
<v Jodi Goodwin>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Jodi Goodwin>[西班牙语]

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:51.340
<v Nadia Reiman>The mama bear is going to come out. We're going to be a mama bear tomorrow. So the next morning, they drive to the shelter armed with paperwork. It looks like a church. There are basketball courts outside, there's classrooms. Jodi and Anita knock on the door, and they go inside. And then, some BCFS employees take them to this room, and there's Jenri. No need for tents or mama bears or anything. They're ready to release them. Anita's thrilled, but her five year old, who hasn't seen her in a month--
<v Nadia Reiman>母狮要出动了。我们明天要变成母狮。第二天早上，她们拿着文件前往收容所。它看起来像一座教堂。外面有篮球场，有教室。乔迪和安妮塔敲开门，走了进去。然后，一些BCFS的员工带她们进了一个房间，詹里就在那里。不需要帐篷或母狮之类的东西。他们准备释放他们了。安妮塔很激动，但她那一个月没见到她的五岁孩子——

00:11:51.340 --> 00:12:33.340
<v Jodi Goodwin>Jenri was sitting in a chair, and he remained seated in the chair. And he didn't get out of the chair. He didn't run to his mom. And that shocked Anita. She started crying, and she knelt down on the floor in front of the chair where her little boy was sitting. And she tried to give him a hug, and she asked for forgiveness a lot of times. [SPEAKING SPANISH] over and over again. And then she kept talking to him and she pulled herself back a little bit so she could see his face.
<v Jodi Goodwin>詹里坐在椅子上，他保持着坐姿。他没有从椅子上下来。他没有跑向他妈妈。这让安妮塔震惊了。她开始哭泣，并在那个小男孩坐的椅子前跪在地上。她试着拥抱他，并多次请求原谅。[西班牙语] 一遍又一遍。然后她继续对他说话，她稍微后退了一点，这样她就能看到他的脸。

00:12:33.340 --> 00:13:04.370
<v Jodi Goodwin>She put her hands on his face, and she asked how he was and if they had treated him well. And he didn't say anything. She kind of just gave it a few seconds to make some eye contact. It was probably about a minute later that he reached his arms around and gave her a big hug, and I think they probably hugged each other for about two minutes before they let go.
<v Jodi Goodwin>她把双手放在他的脸上，问他怎么样，他们对他好不好。他什么也没说。她给了他几秒钟做眼神接触。可能大约一分钟后，他伸出双臂给了她一个大大的拥抱，我想他们大概拥抱了两分钟才分开。

00:13:04.370 --> 00:13:26.690
<v Nadia Reiman>It's not clear why it was so quick to get him out. Jodi's best guess is that it's some combination of all the media attention plus the upcoming deadline for reunifying parents and kids. On top of that, if Anita had set up a tent outside, which she was totally ready to do, it wouldn't have been great press.
<v Nadia Reiman>为什么能这么快接他出来还不清楚。乔迪的最佳猜测是媒体关注加上即将到来的父母与孩子团聚期限的共同作用。最重要的是，如果安妮塔在外面扎了帐篷，而她完全准备好这样做，那将不是什么好的新闻报道。

00:13:26.690 --> 00:13:44.110
<v Nadia Reiman>So all this audio was gathered by my friend, Jeremy Raff, from The Atlantic, and I'm just going to let him mostly take it from here. When Jodi and Anita went to pick up Jenri, he was there, but he wasn't allowed in the shelter. So he waited outside. It was like 100 degrees out, so he was under a tree.
<v Nadia Reiman>所以所有这些录音都是我的朋友，来自《大西洋月刊》的杰里米·拉夫收集的，接下来大部分时间就让他来讲吧。当乔迪和安妮塔去接詹里时，他在那里，但他不能进收容所。所以他在外面等着。当时大约有100华氏度（约38摄氏度），所以他待在一棵树下。

00:13:44.110 --> 00:14:12.520
<v Jeremy Raff>After a long time, I see, first I catch sight of Jodi, and she's walking next to a woman in a sort of starched blue button up with the BCFS logo on it. And then next to her is a really little boy, like smaller than I thought he was going to look like, holding Anita's hand. And the four of them are walking out.
<v Jeremy Raff>过了很长时间，我先看到了乔迪，她正走在一个穿着浆洗过的蓝色扣领衬衫、上面印有BCFS标志的女人旁边。在她旁边是一个很小的男孩，看起来比我预想的还要小，牵着安妮塔的手。他们四个人一起走了出来。

00:14:12.520 --> 00:14:39.480
<v Nadia Reiman>I've seen a bunch of videos and photos online of parents being reunited with their kids, grabbing them like a life raft while the kid looks confused and far away. But they usually stop here. I'd never really heard what happens next, once the parents and kids are left trying to figure out how to be with each other again after weeks of not knowing where the other slept or ate, or when they were coming back. Jeremy is with Anita and Jenri while all of this is happening.
<v Nadia Reiman>我在网上看过很多父母与孩子团聚的视频和照片，父母像抓住救生圈一样抱住孩子，而孩子看起来困惑且疏离。但通常视频到这就结束了。我从没真正听过接下来会发生什么，当父母和孩子在经历了数周不知道对方在哪里睡觉或吃饭，或者不知道对方什么时候回来之后，又要努力重新学会如何在一起生活。这一切发生时，杰里米正和安妮塔与詹里在一起。

00:14:39.480 --> 00:15:00.540
<v Jeremy Raff>They walk up to Jodi's truck and they open the door, and like, you know, Jodi has a kid the same age as this little boy, and so she brought some of his toys along and they're in the back seat. And so he's like checking out these little monster trucks that Jodi brought for him.
<v Jeremy Raff>他们走到乔迪的卡车旁，打开车门，你知道，乔迪有一个和这个小男孩一样大的孩子，所以她带了一些他的玩具放在后座。所以他正在查看乔迪为他带来的这些小怪物卡车。

